data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1: Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?**\n\n**The short version:** carts have outgrown the 4 KB session cookie, so they need to live on the server. The question is which database holds them. The main agent recommends Postgres.\n\n**Why the cookie is no longer enough:** a cart stored in the browser cookie has a hard 4 KB limit. Once a shopper has enough items, the cookie gets too big and the cart breaks or gets dropped. Moving the cart to the server fixes that. Each cart is saved under an ID, and the cookie holds only that ID.\n\n**What each option leads to:**\n\n- **Redis (a1):** carts are kept in memory, so reads and writes are very fast and a cart can expire on its own after a set time. The cost is that Redis is usually treated as a cache. Unless it's set up for persistence, a restart or failure can lose carts. That's a problem if you want to recover abandoned carts or keep a cart history.\n- **Postgres (a2, recommended):** carts are saved to the main database, so they survive restarts and can be queried later, for example to find abandoned carts or to join with orders and customers. The cost is slightly slower reads and writes than Redis, plus one more table and cleanup job to manage. For a typical cart workload, that speed gap is unlikely to be noticed.\n\n**Recommendation:** Postgres, unless the stack already runs Redis and nothing needs to be kept long-term. Carts are business data, and losing them costs real sales. Postgres keeps them durable and easy to query without adding a new service.\n\n**One gap I can't close from here:** the project checkout I can read is empty (it contains only git metadata), so I can't see whether the app already runs Redis or Postgres. If one of them is already in production, that should weigh heavily toward it, since adding a new service has a cost.\n\nA1 is waiting on you. Pick Redis or Postgres on the ask in chat, or tell me here and I'll record it."}}

